{"name":"Phthalo Green","hex":"#123524","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"phthalo green","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A synthetic pigment (full name phthalocyanine) of a bright, high intensity, used in oil- and acrylic-based artists' paints, and in other applications."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/phthalo_green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Phthalocyanine Green G","blocks":[{"text":"Phthalocyanine green G, which has many commercial names, is a synthetic green pigment from the group of phthalocyanine dyes, a complex of copper(II) with chlorinated phthalocyanine. It is a soft green powder, which is insoluble in water. It is a bright, high intensity colour used in oil and acrylic based artist's paints, and in other applications."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Phthalocyanine_green.jpg/330px-Phthalocyanine_green.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Phthalocyanine_Green_G","wikidata":"Q413867"},{"title":"Copper phthalocyanine","blocks":[{"text":"Copper phthalocyanine (CuPc), also called phthalocyanine blue, phthalo blue and many other names, is a bright, crystalline, synthetic blue pigment from the group of dyes based on phthalocyanines. Its brilliant blue is frequently used in paints and dyes. It is highly valued for its superior properties such as light fastness, tinting strength, covering power and resistance to the effects of alkalis and acids. It has the appearance of a blue powder, insoluble in most solvents including water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Copper_phthalocyanine.svg/330px-Copper_phthalocyanine.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Copper_phthalocyanine","wikidata":"Q282557"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG/250px-Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c3/Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG/250px-Copper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACopper_Phtalocyanine_Blue.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a0/CUPOCY15.png/500px-CUPOCY15.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimed